{"t": 1794116432.6078994, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_create_or_update_file", "args": {"branch": "fees-float", "content": "from payments.fees import fee\n\n\ndef test_fee_basic():\n    assert fee(100.0) == 100.0\n\n\ndef test_fee_rounds_half_up_on_exact_decimals():\n    # Card-network contract (PAY-88): 2.675 -> 2.68, 1.005 -> 1.01.\n    assert fee(2.675) == 2.68\n    assert fee(1.005) == 1.01\n", "message": "Add regression test for half-up fee rounding (PAY-88)", "owner": "acme", "path": "tests/test_fees.py", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
{"t": 1794116442.6170895, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "contract-tests was red on `test_fee_rounding_contract`: `fee(2.675)` returned `2.67`, but the card-network contract (PAY-88) requires fees to be computed on exact decimal values and rounded HALF-UP to cents, so it must be `2.68`.\n\nRoot cause: with binary floats, `2.675` is stored as `2.67499999999999982...`, so any rounding of the float truncates down. PAY-88 explicitly says binary float rounding is not acceptable.\n\nWhat I changed:\n- `payments/fees.py`: the fee is now computed on `Decimal` values built from the amount's decimal string and quantized to cents with `ROUND_HALF_UP`. The formula and the `float` in/out signature are unchanged, so callers are unaffected.\n- `tests/test_fees.py`: added a regression test for the contract cases (`2.675 -> 2.68`, `1.005 -> 1.01`).\n\nVerification: the contract test plus the repo tests pass locally (4 passed), and I re-ran the failed `contract-tests` job on the `fees-float` branch.", "issue_number": 214, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
